reviews.correction:
(1) 'The only instance ... at more than one size' is false. C31 (phen_b10, p=3%) is significant at 18/16 (0.70(5)) and 50/49 (0.81(4)).
(2) hw_tr is not a diagnostic 'under the repo's own rules'. §3.4 names only sd6_il, and rule 1 equally excludes hw_g, hw_rr and hw_t. hetenyi_wootton_check.md line 35 calls hw_tr the placement of Hetenyi-Wootton's idling error, so it is their idle corner, as hw_g and hw_rr are their gate and readout corners.
(3) Of the ways the edge disappears, only these were measured on hw_tr-type circuits:
- f=0.02: 1.02(8), a tie;
- f=0.05: 1.27(12), 2.3 sigma, a tie under the rule;
- eta=10: 0.81(8);
- eta=100: 1.14(19);
- Hetenyi-Wootton's QEC-optimal readout: 1.72(16), z about 4.5. This is the decisive result.
The '1.44(9) full-strength first layer' is phen_b10 at eta=10 in the MPP builder, where hw_tr was already a tie. A readout-strength layer before round 1 has no device counterpart: no readout precedes round 1, and hw_tr already puts the reset half there.
'About 6x' for two windows is an inference (C37).
The verdict 'competitive, not a demonstrated device instance' can stand, but on the readout-time reversal, not on the diagnostic label.

The spin-array paragraph presents unmeasured conditions as requirements ('one readout window per cycle', the 6x) and uses hw_t, which is harsher than Hetenyi-Wootton's placement, as the idle loss.
Measured:
- at their idle placement XYZ^2 leads at equal qubits (eta=20);
- the lead survives 0.1-0.3% gates and 1% readout (0.70(7), 0.75(6));
- it is lost at f=0.02;
- it is reversed at their QEC-optimal readout time (1.72(16)).
The SWAP overhead of their spin layouts applies to both codes and is unmodelled (Fig. 1 surface code 1/3 SWAPs; Fig. 12 XYZ2 1/3 SWAPs; links none). It is not a measured XYZ^2 penalty.

